iPhone spatial photos are HEIC files that hold a left-eye and a right-eye image. It would nice to be able to detect these and filter them, because image quality when viewed in 2D is lower than an equivalent non-spatial photo. I'd like:

1. Detection, exposed as photo metadata.
2. A catalog filter (show / hide / only).
3. (Optional) A side-by-side view of the two eyes.

Neither EXIF nor the image size identifies these files. The primary image is a separate monoscopic image, larger than the stereo pair. On an iPhone 16 Pro Max file, each eye is 2688×2016, which is also what EXIF reports. The primary image is 5712×4284, and that is what decoders return.

## How to detect them

A spatial photo is a HEIF file whose meta box holds a stereo-pair entity group (ISO/IEC 23008-12). That is meta → grpl → a ster box with exactly two entities, the left and right item ids. Other groups don't count. For example, altr is on ordinary iPhone HEICs that carry an HDR gain map.

Only box headers and the meta box are read, never image data, so a check costs a few KB of I/O. This is the pure-Rust version I use, with no libheif dependency:

```rust
use std::io::{self, Read, Seek, SeekFrom};

const MAX_META_BYTES: u64 = 16 * 1024 * 1024;

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct StereoPair { pub left: u32, pub right: u32 }

pub fn stereo_pair<R: Read + Seek>(r: &mut R) -> io::Result<Option<StereoPair>> {
    let end = r.seek(SeekFrom::End(0))?;
    let mut pos = 0u64;
    let mut first = true;
    while pos + 8 <= end {
        r.seek(SeekFrom::Start(pos))?;
        let Some((kind, header, size)) = read_header(r, end - pos)? else { break };
        // Every HEIF file starts with `ftyp`; anything else is not HEIF.
        if first && &kind != b"ftyp" { return Ok(None); }
        first = false;
        if &kind == b"meta" {
            let len = size - header;
